{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>For the border of the children of Ammon was strong.</b> His border was strong and mighty, and what is the strength? The warning of the Holy One, blessed be He, who said to Israel: do not besiege them. And this is what it says: 'For Heshbon was the city of Sihon king of the Amorites, and he fought against the first king of Moab and took all his land from his hand as far as the Arnon' — this teaches you that Heshbon had belonged to the dominion of Moab, and since Israel were not permitted to wage war against Moab because of what Scripture warned (Deuteronomy 2:9), 'Do not besiege Moab,' therefore it informed us that Sihon took it from them, and Israel took it from him, as it is written (Joshua 13:25), 'and half the land of the children of Ammon' — and they took it from the hand of the Amorite who had first taken it from the hand of the children of Ammon. And what Scripture said (Deuteronomy 2:5) concerning Ammon and Moab — that HaShem would not give Israel any of their land even to the tread of a foot — means the land that was in their possession at that time; and this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Ammon and Moab were rendered permissible through Sihon,' that is, they were rendered fit for Israel through Sihon, for it came to them lawfully from the hand of Sihon. Therefore Scripture needed to cite the words of the parable-sayers concerning Heshbon that Israel took, for so Scripture says: 'And Israel dwelt in all the cities of the Amorites, in Heshbon and in all its daughters.' And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, spoke of the extraordinary might of King Sihon and the strength of the city of Heshbon, as they expounded: 'If Heshbon had been full of gnats, no creature could have conquered it; and if Sihon had been in a valley, no creature could have prevailed against him' — and no need to say that Sihon, dwelling in Heshbon, was a great mighty warrior residing in a fortress city. And this is what Scripture mentioned below: 'to Sihon king of the Amorites, who dwelt in Heshbon,' and they expounded on it: why was his name called Sihon? Because all would converse (מסיחין) about his might. And concerning the son of Sihon as well, our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said that he was a great mighty warrior, as they expounded: 'and they struck him and his sons' — 'his son' is written, meaning he had a son who was harder than he."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:45.745032657Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}